1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,439
Pager, 02:10, seaport gate API. Containers
are "Running." Clients time out. On-call
curls from the jump host: Look at this
bash for a moment. JSON returns
"status":"DOWN" with db detail Connection
refused.

2
00:00:13,559 --> 00:00:23,559
Not a mystery novel — Actuator turned a
black box into a health document. The
outage was a drained connection pool after
a Postgres failover the app never learned
about.

3
00:00:23,679 --> 00:00:29,000
Without that endpoint, the next step would
have been speculative pod deletes and a
debugger on a live gate.

4
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:44,560
Spring Boot Actuator exposes operational
endpoints: health, info, metrics, env
(careful), loggers, heapdump (very
careful), and more. Add spring-boot-
starter-actuator. Secure them.

5
00:00:44,679 --> 00:00:55,519
In production, expose only what ops needs,
often on a separate management port so
public traffic and ops traffic do not
share the same accidental exposure. Look
at this example.

6
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:12,200
management: Look at this java for a
moment. Walk the config and indicator.
management.server.port: 8081 binds
actuators away from the main 8080 API
port. exposure.include is an allowlist —
nothing else is web-exposed.

7
00:01:12,319 --> 00:01:17,679
show-details: when_authorized avoids
leaking DB URLs to anonymous callers.

8
00:01:17,799 --> 00:01:28,479
TideApiHealthIndicator is a custom check:
health() pings the tide service the gate
depends on for clearance windows; UP/DOWN
with details aggregates into the overall
document.

9
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:34,759
Built-in DB indicators check the
DataSource similarly. Runtime of
/actuator/health.

10
00:01:34,879 --> 00:01:46,840
A web endpoint (management context if
configured) invokes HealthEndpoint, which
aggregates HealthIndicator /
ReactiveHealthIndicator beans — DB, disk
space, custom tide API.

11
00:01:46,959 --> 00:01:59,919
Each returns UP, DOWN, OUT_OF_SERVICE, or
UNKNOWN with optional details. Aggregate
status follows severity rules (DOWN wins
over UP).

12
00:02:00,039 --> 00:02:13,879
Kubernetes can probe
/actuator/health/liveness and
/actuator/health/readiness separately so a
failing dependency marks the app *unready*
(stop sending traffic) without killing the
JVM if you split indicators carefully —

13
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,960
a dependency blip should not always
restart the pod.

14
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:26,120
The metrics endpoint exposes Micrometer
meters; with prometheus on the include
list, scrapers pull time series from the
same process that just answered health.

15
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:36,480
Failure mode from 02:10: health DOWN on
db, pods still "Running" in Kubernetes
because liveness was a TCP port check, not
readiness on actuator.

16
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:46,000
Symptom: Service still routes traffic to
broken pods; clients time out; actuator on
8081 tells the truth. Fix probes to use
readiness.

17
00:02:46,120 --> 00:03:00,600
Another failure: exposing /actuator/env
and /actuator/heapdump on the public app
port without auth — attackers read secrets
or trigger expensive dumps. Symptom may be
security findings or sudden CPU spikes
from heapdump.

18
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:14,960
Lock down include, use management port,
require auth. Trade-offs. Actuator
shortens incidents by making process state
HTTP-queryable; every endpoint is also an
attack and load surface.

19
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:26,120
Fine-grained health indicators improve
signal and can flap readiness if they
check unstable dependencies — sometimes a
dependency belongs in metrics/alerts, not
in readiness.

20
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:33,480
Prefer few, meaningful indicators over a
dozen remote pings on every probe. Walk
the 02:10 curl one layer deeper.

21
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:45,200
HealthEndpoint does not open JDBC itself;
it asks the db indicator, which borrows a
connection from the DataSource and runs a
validation query (or relies on pool
validation).

22
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:57,000
Connection refused means the TCP path to
Postgres failed — host, port, or network
policy — not "Spring is down." Custom
TideApiHealthIndicator runs in the same
aggregation pass;

23
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:05,960
if tide is DOWN but you configured it only
for details and kept readiness on
liveness-separate groups, you can still
serve gate traffic while alerting on tide.

24
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:11,120
That knobs-and-groups design is why
Actuator health is a model, not a single
boolean.

25
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:25,440
Misconception unique to Actuator:
"Exposing /actuator/env and
/actuator/heapdump on the public app port
is fine because Actuator is 'ops only'."
Actuator endpoints are HTTP APIs.

26
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:39,040
Unsecured, they leak configuration and
enable denial-of-service. Management port,
auth, and tight include lists are part of
the feature — not optional manners. Health
shows UP again after Postgres recovers.

27
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:51,360
Developers fixing the museum ticket UI
still restart the whole Boot app for every
Thymeleaf tweak. The feedback loop hurts —
DevTools exists for that loop, with its
own sharp edges.
